.TH VIDENC 1
.SH NAME
videnc, vidjpeg \- a video feed's frames as JPEG between machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount -a {videnc
.I feed
.B }
.I feed
.br
.B mount {vidjpeg
.I feed
.B } /mnt/video
.SH DESCRIPTION
A feed in the schema of
.IR camsrv (4)
and vid9p
.RB ( ctl ,
.BR fmt ,
.BR frame ,
.BR status ,
.BR layer/ )
serves raw I420 frames: a 480x270 frame is 194 KB, a 6 frame a second
feed 1.2 MB a second for each viewer.
.I Videnc
and
.I vidjpeg
compress the frames on the hop between machines and leave both ends
raw, so neither the feed nor what reads it changes:
.I videnc
beside the feed, on its machine;
.I vidjpeg
on the viewer's.
.PP
.I Videnc
reads
.IB feed /fmt
and
.IB feed /frame
and serves a directory
.BR jpeg ,
union-mounted after the feed
.RB ( "mount -a" ,
as
.IR bind (1)
describes)
so that it appears beside
.BR frame :
.TF jpeg/index
.PD
.TP
.B jpeg/ctl
Written
.BI quality " n"
(1 to 100, default 75) sets the quality of the frames encoded from then
on; read, it gives the quality.  A change starts the encoded frames
again.
.TP
.B jpeg/fmt
.BI jpeg " w h " "420 " fps
.TP
.B jpeg/index
Frame
.IR N 's
record, at offset
.IR N *32:
its offset and length in
.BR data ,
as decimal numbers, padded with blanks to 31 bytes and ended by a
newline.  A read must begin at a record.
.TP
.B jpeg/data
The encoded frames end to end, each a baseline JPEG
.RI ( jpegenc (2)),
at offsets that only grow.
.PD
.PP
A frame is encoded when its record is first read, once however many
read it, so a feed nobody reads encoded costs nothing.  A record for a
frame the feed has not made yet waits for it, as
.B frame
waits at a live feed's edge.  A frame the feed no longer keeps, or one
that has left
.IR videnc 's
last 64, is
.BR "frame expired" ;
a frame past a clip's end is refused.
With
.IR export (4)
in front, a remote viewer reads
.B jpeg/
where a local one reads
.BR frame .
.PP
.I Vidjpeg
reads a feed's
.BR jpeg/ ,
local or mounted from another machine, and serves
.BR 0/ctl ,
.BR 0/fmt ,
.BR 0/frame ,
.B 0/status
and
.B 0/layer/
as vid9p does: frame
.I N
is the
.I framesize
bytes at
.IR N * framesize ,
decoded to I420 with
.IR jpegenc (2)'s
.B planes
and no RGB between.
.IR wm-camera (1),
vidplay9p and
.IR vision (4)
read it unchanged.
A frame read in several pieces, as viewers read one, is decoded once.
.B status
is the feed's, read through;
.B ctl
is passed to the feed's, so the playhead is driven as before;
.B layer/
is empty, as vid9p serves it, for layers bound on the viewer.
The feed's errors, such as
.BR "frame expired" ,
come through as they are.
.PP
JPEG at quality 75 makes the frames of a busy street scene about a sixth
of their raw size; a smooth or still scene shrinks further.  Encoding a
480x270 frame takes about 25 ms on a Raspberry Pi 5 under the JIT
compiler, so a feed at 6 frames a second costs it about a sixth of one
core.  A single reader on a fast link reads raw frames faster than JPEG
ones, since each waits for its encoding: these are for links, or numbers
of viewers, where the bandwidth is the limit.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the machine with the feed:
.IP
.EX
mount {vid9p -w 10 -c vdec cam.mp4 --y4m -} /mnt/video
mount -a {videnc /mnt/video/0} /mnt/video/0
listen -a aes_256_gcm tcp!*!17052 {export /mnt/video}
.EE
.PP
On the viewer:
.IP
.EX
mount -C aes_256_gcm tcp!pi5!17052 /n/pi5
mount {vidjpeg /n/pi5/0} /mnt/video
wm/camera
.EE
.PP
Lower the quality from either machine:
.IP
.EX
echo quality 40 > /n/pi5/0/jpeg/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/videnc.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/vidjpeg.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR jpegenc (2),
.IR camsrv (4),
.IR export (4),
.IR vision (4),
.IR wm-camera (1)
.SH BUGS
Frames are coded one by one (JPEG), not from each other as a video
codec codes them, so a still scene costs as much as a moving one.
.PP
The quality is one setting for every viewer of a
.IR videnc ;
for two, run two.
